{
 "id": "unit-circle-coordinates",
 "title": "Unit-circle coordinates and arc distance t",
 "summary": "Find the terminal point P(t) on the unit circle for any real number t and read off sin t and cos t.",
 "objectives": [
  "Find terminal points for the special values of t",
  "Use reference numbers and quadrant signs",
  "Use x² + y² = 1 to find a missing coordinate"
 ],
 "prerequisites": [
  "degree-radian-conversion",
  "exact-trig-values"
 ],
 "lesson": [
  {
   "id": "ucc-b01",
   "type": "text",
   "md": "# The unit circle and arc distance $t$\n\nWrap the real number line around the circle $x^2+y^2=1$, starting at $(1,0)$. A real number $t$ lands at its **terminal point** $P(t)=(x,y)$ after traveling a distance $|t|$ along the circle: counterclockwise if $t>0$, clockwise if $t<0$. Because the radius is $1$, the arc length $t$ is also the angle in radians."
  },
  {
   "id": "ucc-b02",
   "type": "callout",
   "kind": "definition",
   "title": "Sine and cosine of a real number",
   "md": "For any real number $t$ with terminal point $P(t)=(x,y)$:\n\n$\\cos t=x,\\qquad \\sin t=y,\\qquad \\tan t=\\dfrac{y}{x}\\ (x\\neq0).$"
  },
