  {
   "id": "ucc-b07",
   "type": "text",
   "md": "## Reference numbers\n\nFor $t$ outside $\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$, use the **reference number** $\\bar t$: the shortest distance along the circle from $P(t)$ to the $x$-axis. The coordinates of $P(t)$ are those of $P(\\bar t)$, with the signs of its quadrant."
  },
  {
   "id": "ucc-b08",
   "type": "example",
   "title": "$P(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6})$",
   "problem": "Find the terminal point for $t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "md": "$\\frac{\\pi}{2}<\\frac{5\\pi}{6}<\\pi$, so $P(t)$ is in Quadrant II."
    },
    {
     "md": "Reference number: $\\bar t=\\pi-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, and $P(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\frac12)$."
    },
    {
     "md": "In Quadrant II, $x<0$ and $y>0$: $P(\\frac{5\\pi}{6})=(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\frac12)$."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2},\\dfrac12)$"
  },
  {
   "id": "ucc-b09",
   "type": "example",
   "title": "$P(-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4})$",
   "problem": "Find the terminal point for $t=-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{4}$.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "md": "Add $2\\pi$: $-\\frac{7\\pi}{4}+2\\pi=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ has the same terminal point.",
     "note": "Adding or subtracting $2\\pi$ is one full lap: the point does not move."
    },
    {
     "md": "So the point is the same as for $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$: $P(-\\frac{7\\pi}{4})=(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2})$, in Quadrant I."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$(\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2})$"
  },
